<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyeon Hwon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/867">867</a>–<a href="/years/936">936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Kyŏn Hwŏn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyeon Hwon was a Silla soldier who rebelled in 892 and founded Later Baekje in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, opening the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>. He sacked the Silla capital in 927. Deposed and imprisoned by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-singeom">Singeom</a> in 935, he escaped to Goryeo and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> destroy Later Baekje in 936.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most accounts make him the eldest of six children of Ajagae, a farmer and rebel leader surnamed Yi, born in what is now Gaeun-eup in Mungyeong; he later changed his surname to Kyŏn, perhaps to start afresh as a founder. Legends say he grew up drinking a tiger's milk. Under Queen Jinseong, Silla was torn by corruption and famine, and his father led a peasant revolt based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangju">Sangju</a>. Kyŏn Hwŏn is said to have joined the Silla army at fifteen and to have risen to command its forces in the Jeolla region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 892 he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Wansanju</a> and Mujinju, the old lands of Baekje, whose people were hostile to Silla, and in 900 he declared himself king with his capital at Wansanju, saying he would avenge the destruction of Baekje by Silla and Tang in 660. He fought Kung Ye of Later Goguryeo, failing to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naju">Naju</a> from Wang Kŏn in 910. Kung Ye was overthrown in 918, and Wang Kŏn founded Goryeo. Peace with Goryeo, sealed by an exchange of hostages, broke down in 926 when Kyŏn killed the hostage Wang Shin after his own nephew's death.</p><p>Attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> in 927, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongae-of-silla">King Gyeongae</a>, forced him to kill himself, raped his chief consort and let his men assault the royal concubines, then enthroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongsun-of-silla">Gyeongsun of Silla</a>. On the way home he defeated Wang Kŏn, who escaped only through the sacrifice of his general Sin Sung-gyŏm and Kim Nak. Later Baekje never recovered from its heavy defeat at the Battle of Gochang in 930, and a further attack on Goryeo failed in 934. Kyŏn was confirmed as ruler by Wuyue and Later Tang, sought an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> and sent envoys to Heian Japan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His eldest son Sin-gŏm, passed over in favour of Kŭm-gang, killed his brother and confined his father in the temple of Geumsansa, but Kyŏn Hwŏn escaped to Wang Kŏn, who gave him land and slaves. After the Goryeo army destroyed Later Baekje, he died the same year of an inflamed tumour. His reputation has been largely negative, shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> of Kim Bu-sik, whose work historians note has a pro-Silla bias. Through his daughter Lady Kuktae his descendants married into the Goryeo royal house.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyŏn Hwŏn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rebel leaders <b>Gyeon Hwon</b> and Gung Ye carved out Later Baekje (from 892, proclaimed in 900) and Later Goguryeo (901), later renamed Taebong, reducing Silla to the area around Gyeongju in the period known as the Later Three Kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gyeon Hwon</b>, a Silla soldier, rose in revolt in 892 amid peasant uprisings against the weakened Silla court and seized much of the southwest.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-baekje">Later Baekje</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mungyeong, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.59,128.2&amp;year=911" aria-label="Mungyeong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7771 844 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gyeon Hwon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8221 1114h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8221 1114h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.59,128.2&amp;year=911">What was here in 911 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
